00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.610
Now I would like to delve deeper
into the Iron Mountain story.

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:38.169
I want to talk about
Iron Mountain's

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:41.619
business and technical drivers
for this and future projects.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:46.220
Global operations
has a requirement

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:51.150
to realize cost savings in
record center operations.

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.550
There's a need for
productivity monitoring

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:58.620
in record centers across
the Iron Mountain footprint.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.640
There is a need for consistency
in building and activity

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:06.500
definitions across countries
and operational systems.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:09.570
There is a need for visibility
into facility or building level

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:13.100
information.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:15.290
There's a lack of clear
insight for leaders

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:17.900
across multiple sites,
regions, and countries

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.498
that needs to be addressed.

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:26.310
There are mostly file-based
processes with challenges

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:30.040
related to file transfers.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.620
There is a monolithic
data source scape

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.360
with challenges
related to reference

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:39.490
data definition for districts,
buildings, and activities.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.860
There are time
consuming manual checks

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:43.675
with reference data
provided by business.

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:50.080
The reports are defined in
crystal reports provision

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:51.490
through business objects.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.970
There is a need to
standardize reporting tools.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:58.760
Now I would like to talk
about the architecture

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:00.620
of the solution we designed.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:07.870
Some important features to note,
an on-premise virtual machine

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:11.300
to upload the data extracts
from multiple data sources

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:17.070
to Google Cloud Storage; file
upload event-based invocation

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:22.160
of Google Cloud
Composer DAG; upload

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:28.340
of data to BigQuery staging
tables orchestrated by DAG;

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:30.950
invocation of SQL
transformations

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:34.530
and merging of data orchestrated
by DAG into BigQuery

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:39.940
and reporting data set;
and finally, RCO reporting

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:43.120
dashboard that we built
on Looker which I'll

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.620
present on the later slide.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:48.930
The relevant
scalability principles

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:54.190
in play for this
engagement are BigQuery,

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:57.580
a highly scalable
serverless data warehouse

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:01.940
setup with on-demand
usage and growth;

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:05.600
recommendation for automatic
scaling of Composer worker

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.290
nodes based on usage metrics;
and finally, an event

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:13.410
driven ingestion pattern
we've built with discussion

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:18.700
around moving to message
driven Cloud Pub/Sub in future.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:22.870
With that, I would like now to
talk about the key TCP products

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:25.390
and services we implemented
on this project.

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:28.090
Google Cloud Composer for
workflow orchestration

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:31.897
that included processing and
loading files into tables

00:11:31.897 --> 00:11:33.730
and running secret
transformations and mulch

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:34.230
queries.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:40.660
BigQuery for loading, coding,
and analyzing large amounts

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.790
of data cost effectively.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:47.910
Google Storage for staging
and archiving of file

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:50.670
extracts in CSV
formats, facilitating

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.572
future retrieval if necessary.

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:58.610
Google Operations or
Stackdriver for logging,

00:11:58.610 --> 00:12:00.400
monitoring, and
alerting activities

00:12:00.400 --> 00:12:03.680
of the ingestion pipeline.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:05.540
And finally,
delivering insights,

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:08.540
reports, and dashboards
using Looker.

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:11.430
I would like to spend a few
minutes talking about business

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:13.770
intelligence platform,
Looker, and how

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:17.210
it benefited Iron Mountain.

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:19.160
Implementing Looker
helped us quickly

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:23.300
deliver insights and enable
real time analytics in a matter

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.590
of two to three weeks.

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:30.150
It helped us implement
governance and data integrity

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:34.690
by limiting table
access, data access,

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:37.390
masking specific
columns, and auditing.

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:43.270
Having the self-service feature
enable the business analysts

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:49.000
to ask new questions about the
data that is readily available,

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.250
and to troubleshoot
problematic indicators

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:52.810
with Iron Mountain operations.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:59.890
And most importantly, Looker
leverages the GCP investment

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:01.810
by Iron Mountain.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:05.800
It basically leverages
BigQuery natively

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:06.925
to perform all the queries.

00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:13.010
What I'm now presenting is
the record center operations

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:17.610
dashboard that we developed
for Iron Mountain.

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:20.400
Some of the insights
the dashboard enables

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:25.480
are the ability to investigate
dip in achievement percentage

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:28.290
by drilling to specific
country, and district,

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:32.640
and down to the warehouse;
collaborate with country

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:37.540
managers to identify
specific bottlenecks

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:40.870
and take specific actions
to bolster the workforce

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:46.500
or take corrective actions
within the same day;

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:50.290
ability to explore the data
and ask additional questions,

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:53.430
especially why some buildings
are reporting high overtime

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:58.680
hours, for example;
set up alerts

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.770
when achievement thresholds
are not met so quick action can

00:14:01.770 --> 00:14:05.340
be initiated.

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:08.070
Some of the challenges we
found and the recommendations

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:11.720
we were able to
provide are agreeing

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:15.020
upon data formats, business
logic, and structure

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:20.230
ahead of time to minimize
re-engineering, continue

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:23.530
to develop a diverse culture
for maximum scalability

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:27.190
and portability by leveraging
terraform for automated

00:14:27.190 --> 00:14:32.320
deployments, and building a
CICD pipeline for efficient code

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:33.040
deployments.

00:14:36.130 --> 00:14:38.500
Data cleansing is
best done upstream,

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.440
near the source of the
data, to minimize complexity

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:42.430
in coding logic.

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:49.150
Review architectural decisions
constantly and optimize

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:51.800
to implement the right fit.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:54.050
For example, Cloud
Dataflow was determined

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:57.410
to be an overkill for the
simple transformations.

00:14:57.410 --> 00:15:01.280
We switched to using BQ Load,
as there were minimal or no SQL

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:04.340
transformations, or the parallel
processing wasn't needed.

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:11.420
Identified growth opportunities.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:14.200
We recommended improved
security monitoring,

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:19.250
firewall logs, alerts, and
so on, fault resolution,

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:23.015
and performance management
for future projects.

00:15:26.087 --> 00:15:27.670
And finally, I would
like to close out

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:32.190
with the project outcomes
we were able to achieve.

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:34.410
We completed proof of
concept to ingest data

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:37.740
from Iron Mountain systems to
BigQuery for data analysis.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:45.420
We enabled enterprise data
warehouse modernization

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:49.990
and reporting through
data democratization.

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:53.200
Enabled near real-time
handling of data ingestion

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:57.920
and on-demand visualization.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:01.280
Helped reduce different tools
being used at Iron Mountain

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:03.790
to reduce copying of data sets.

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.380
Enabled iron
mountain for success

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:11.590
by beginning to
create a unified GCP

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:15.210
standard across
the organization,

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:18.900
and established an
infrastructure baseline

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:22.410
for future data ingestion
and visualization projects.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:25.150
This concludes our presentation.

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:27.300
JARRETT GARCIA: Thank you.

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:29.260
HARISH KURAM: Thanks, everyone.

